        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>We&#x27;ve already seen that <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5^2]</span> means <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5\\cdot 5,]</span> and that <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5^3]</span> means <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5.]</span> We can extend this to have any number in the exponent, for example\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5^{\\color{blue}7} = \\underbrace{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5}_{\\large{\\color{blue}7} \\textrm{ copies of } 5}.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "We've already seen that [MATH: 5^2] means [MATH: 5\\cdot 5,] and that [MATH: 5^3] means [MATH: 5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5.] We can extend this to have any number in the exponent, for example\n[MATH: 5^{\\color{blue}7} = \\underbrace{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5}_{\\large{\\color{blue}7} \\textrm{ copies of } 5}.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The <strong>exponent</strong> <span class=\"math\">[MATH: ({\\color{blue}7})]</span> says how many times the <strong>base</strong> <span class=\"math\">[MATH: (5)]</span> is multiplied by itself.</p>",
            "readable_text": "The exponent [MATH: ({\\color{blue}7})] says how many times the base [MATH: (5)] is multiplied by itself.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Recall that <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5^2]</span> is said as &quot;five squared&quot; and that <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5^3]</span> is &quot;five cubed.&quot; We don&#x27;t have special names for larger exponents. However, we can say <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5^7]</span>  in several different ways:</p>",
            "readable_text": "Recall that [MATH: 5^2] is said as \"five squared\" and that [MATH: 5^3] is \"five cubed.\" We don't have special names for larger exponents. However, we can say [MATH: 5^7] in several different ways:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<ul><li><p>&quot;five raised to the seventh power,&quot;</p></li><li><p>&quot;five raised to the power seven,&quot; </p></li><li><p>&quot;five to the seventh power,&quot; </p></li><li><p>&quot;five to the power seven.&quot;</p></li></ul>",
            "readable_text": "- \"five raised to the seventh power,\"\n- \"five raised to the power seven,\"\n- \"five to the seventh power,\"\n- \"five to the power seven.\"",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Raising to the power of one is special. Any number raised to the first power is itself. For example, <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5^{\\color{blue}1}]</span> means one copy of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5,]</span> so <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5^{\\color{blue}1} = 5.]</span> </p>",
            "readable_text": "Raising to the power of one is special. Any number raised to the first power is itself. For example, [MATH: 5^{\\color{blue}1}] means one copy of [MATH: 5,] so [MATH: 5^{\\color{blue}1} = 5.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nWe've already seen that [MATH: 5^2] means [MATH: 5\\cdot 5,] and that [MATH: 5^3] means [MATH: 5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5.] We can extend this to have any number in the exponent, for example\n[MATH: 5^{\\color{blue}7} = \\underbrace{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5}_{\\large{\\color{blue}7} \\textrm{ copies of } 5}.]\n\nContent:\nThe exponent [MATH: ({\\color{blue}7})] says how many times the base [MATH: (5)] is multiplied by itself.\n\nContent:\nRecall that [MATH: 5^2] is said as \"five squared\" and that [MATH: 5^3] is \"five cubed.\" We don't have special names for larger exponents. However, we can say [MATH: 5^7] in several different ways:\n\nContent:\n- \"five raised to the seventh power,\"\n- \"five raised to the power seven,\"\n- \"five to the seventh power,\"\n- \"five to the power seven.\"\n\nContent:\nRaising to the power of one is special. Any number raised to the first power is itself. For example, [MATH: 5^{\\color{blue}1}] means one copy of [MATH: 5,] so [MATH: 5^{\\color{blue}1} = 5.]"
      },
