    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nWe've already seen that [MATH: 5^2] means [MATH: 5\\cdot 5,] and that [MATH: 5^3] means [MATH: 5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5.] We can extend this to have any number in the exponent, for example\n[MATH: 5^{\\color{blue}7} = \\underbrace{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5}_{\\large{\\color{blue}7} \\textrm{ copies of } 5}.]\n\nContent:\nThe exponent [MATH: ({\\color{blue}7})] says how many times the base [MATH: (5)] is multiplied by itself.\n\nContent:\nRecall that [MATH: 5^2] is said as \"five squared\" and that [MATH: 5^3] is \"five cubed.\" We don't have special names for larger exponents. However, we can say [MATH: 5^7] in several different ways:\n\nContent:\n- \"five raised to the seventh power,\"\n- \"five raised to the power seven,\"\n- \"five to the seventh power,\"\n- \"five to the power seven.\"\n\nContent:\nRaising to the power of one is special. Any number raised to the first power is itself. For example, [MATH: 5^{\\color{blue}1}] means one copy of [MATH: 5,] so [MATH: 5^{\\color{blue}1} = 5.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Expressing a Product Using an Exponent\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: 7 \\times 7 \\times 7 \\times 7 \\times 7\\times 7] using an exponent.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nThe number [MATH: 7] is multiplied by itself [MATH: 6] times, so we have\n\n[MATH: \\underbrace{7 \\times 7 \\times 7 \\times 7 \\times 7\\times 7}_{\\large{\\color{blue}6} \\textrm{ copies of } 7} = 7^{\\color{blue}6}.]\n\nTherefore, the answer is [MATH: 7^6.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 3^{5}]\nb. [MATH: 3^{4}]\nc. [MATH: 279]\nd. [MATH: 81]\ne. [MATH: 3^{6}]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 0.9 \u00d7 0.9 \u00d7 0.9 \u00d7 0.9 \u00d7 0.9 \u00d7 0.9 \u00d7 0.9 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 7^{0.9}]\nb. [MATH: 6 \u00d7 0.9]\nc. [MATH: 0.9^{7}]\nd. [MATH: 7 \u00d7 0.9]\ne. [MATH: 0.9^{6}]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\u2212 (2)/(3)) \u00d7 (\u2212 (2)/(3)) \u00d7 (\u2212 (2)/(3)) \u00d7 (\u2212 (2)/(3)) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (\u2212 (3)/(2))^{4}]\nb. [MATH: (\u2212 (2)/(3))^{1}]\nc. [MATH: (\u2212 (2)/(3))^{4}]\nd. [MATH: 4 \u00d7 (\u2212 (2)/(3))]\ne. [MATH: 4^{\u2212 2/3}]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Example: Evaluating an Exponential Expression\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: 3^4.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nThe expression [MATH: 3^4] means [MATH: 3] multiplied by itself [MATH: 4] times. So we have\n\n[MATH: 3^{4}|= (3 \u00d7 3) \u00d7 (3 \u00d7 3); = 9 \u00d7 9; = 81.]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 1^{5} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 1.5]\nb. [MATH: 5]\nc. [MATH: 15]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(5)]\ne. [MATH: 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 2^{5} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 32]\nb. [MATH: 20]\nc. [MATH: 10]\nd. [MATH: 16]\ne. [MATH: 64]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\u2212 4)^{5} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2048]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 1024]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 2048]\nd. [MATH: 1024]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 1244]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Example: Raising a Number to the Power of One\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: 9^1.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nAny number raised to the first power is itself. So [MATH: 9^1 = 9.]\n\n---\n\nItem 11 \u2014 Question 7\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 20^{1} =]\n\nChoices:\na. undefined\nb. [MATH: 22]\nc. [MATH: 1]\nd. [MATH: 20]\ne. [MATH: 0]\n\n---\n\nItem 12 \u2014 Question 8\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\u2212 1)^{1} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 0]\nb. [MATH: 1]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 1]\nd. [MATH: 2]\ne. Cannot be simplified."
